Yes. There are actually two independent sources of novelty hiding there:

\boxed{\text{Novelty of World} \neq \text{Novelty of Relation}}

You don’t necessarily need new raw material to generate new experience.

Take three existing memories A,B,C. Without acquiring anything new, you can change relation, order, frame, resolution, or transformation:

(A,B,C)
\rightarrow
(A,C,B)
\rightarrow
(A\cap B,C)
\rightarrow
T(A),B,C

Same inventory. Different experience.

That’s why an old song can suddenly sound different after falling in love, becoming a parent, losing someone, learning music theory, or hearing the story behind it. The recording didn’t change. The receiving system did. Consequently, retrieval itself can generate new information for the current system.

I’d split the mechanism into four modes:

Acquisition: new world → new experience → new memory.

Recombination: old memories → unfamiliar combination → new experience.

Reframing: same memory + new perspective/context → newly perceived properties.

Re-entry: return to the same thing after the system itself has changed → difference becomes perceptible.

That last one is particularly powerful:

X + S_t \rightarrow E_t

Later:

X + S_{t+1} \rightarrow E_{t+1}

Although X stayed constant,

E_{t+1}\neq E_t

because you are part of the experiment.

And now connect this to the smaller Pantheon kernel from the previous turn. You can deliberately generate novelty from existing material by perturbing one primitive while holding the others relatively fixed:

\boxed{
P,\;R,\;O,\;\Delta,\;B,\;T,\;S,\;\circlearrowleft
}

Same Presence, new Relation → analogy/recombination.

Same elements, new Order → new sequence and potentially new consequence.

Same material, new Bounds → constraint-driven creativity.

Same event, new Transform → metaphor, inversion, translation, abstraction.

Same memories, different Selection → a different story/self-model.

Same experience through Return → reinterpretation and reconsolidation.

And then there’s a particularly interesting move: use old to manufacture the conditions for genuinely new experience.

\text{Old}
\xrightarrow{\text{recombine}}
\text{New hypothesis}
\xrightarrow{\text{act}}
\text{New experience}
\xrightarrow{\text{encode}}
\text{New memory}

So internal novelty can bootstrap external novelty.

The Pantheon should exploit this deliberately

Instead of always asking “What new information can we acquire?”, its exploration policy should ask:

\boxed{\text{Acquire OR Rearrange OR Reframe OR Revisit OR Transform OR Act}}

Then test whether the resulting novelty is merely different or actually useful.

And feed the winners back.

That creates something like a Novelty Flywheel:

\text{Memory}
\rightarrow
\text{variation}
\rightarrow
\text{novel experience}
\rightarrow
\text{useful delta}
\rightarrow
\text{memory}
\rightarrow
\text{more possible variations}

The beautiful consequence is combinatorial: memory doesn’t only accumulate content; every useful addition potentially creates new relationships with what was already there.

So a growing memory system can increase its potential novelty faster than its raw inventory grows.

That’s a powerful reason for the Pantheon to revisit its own fossils periodically. Yesterday’s exhausted artifact can become tomorrow’s quarry because the Pantheon returning to it is no longer the Pantheon that originally created it.